window.PQ_SAMPLE = {"igcse":{"name":"IGCSE Computer Science","code":"0478 / 0984","lessonTotal":155,"questionTotal":655,"lessons":[{"id":"1.1.1","code":"1.1.1","title":"Why computers use binary","video":{"id":"pol5ado7YCc","label":"Video: number systems"},"concept":"<p>A computer is built from billions of tiny electronic switches. A switch has only two states, <b>on</b> or <b>off</b>, so a computer can only work with two values. We write these two values as <b>1</b> (on) and <b>0</b> (off). This is <b>binary</b>.</p><p>Because of this, <b>any form of data</b> (numbers, text, sound, images and even the program instructions) has to be <b>converted to binary</b> before the computer can process it. Inside the computer the binary data is processed using <b>logic gates</b> and is stored in <b>registers</b>.</p>","example":"<p>The letter <b>A</b> cannot be stored as a letter. It is stored as a binary pattern, for example <code>01000001</code>.</p><ul><li>A photo is stored as a long list of binary numbers, one for the colour of each pixel.</li><li>A song is stored as a long list of binary numbers measuring the sound wave.</li><li>The instruction to add two numbers is also stored as binary.</li></ul>","checks":[{"q":"Why do computers use binary to represent data?","options":["Its electronic parts have only two states, on and off","Binary numbers take less memory than denary numbers, so more data fits","Humans find binary easier to read and write than denary numbers","Binary is the only number system that a CPU is able to add together"],"correct":0,"explain":"Electronic switches have two states (on/off), which are written as 1 and 0. Everything the computer handles must be turned into these two values."},{"q":"A student saves a photograph and a song on a computer. What must happen to this data before the computer can process it?","options":["It is encrypted","It is converted to binary","It is converted to hexadecimal","It is compressed into text"],"correct":1,"explain":"Any form of data, including images and sound, must be converted to binary before it can be processed."}]},{"id":"2.1.1b","code":"2.1.1","title":"Packet switching","video":null,"concept":"<p><b>Packet switching</b> is a method of sending packets across a network. Each packet is sent <b>individually</b> and each packet may take a <b>different pathway</b> to the destination.</p><p>A <b>router</b> controls this. When a packet reaches a router, the router reads the <b>destination address</b> in the header and decides which pathway to send the packet along next. This repeats at every router until the packet reaches the destination.</p><p>Because packets travel different routes, they often <b>arrive out of order</b>. When all packets have arrived, the receiving device uses the <b>packet numbers</b> to <b>reorder</b> them and recreate the file.</p>","example":"<p>Five packets go from device A to device B through routers 1 to 5.</p><ul><li>Packet 1: Router 1 &rarr; Router 2</li><li>Packet 2: Router 1 &rarr; Router 4 &rarr; Router 5</li><li>Packet 3: Router 1 &rarr; Router 3 &rarr; Router 2</li></ul><p>Packet 2 might arrive after packet 3, because its route was different. Device B waits for all five packets, then uses the packet numbers (1 to 5) to put them in order.</p>","checks":[{"q":"In packet switching, which device decides the pathway each packet takes next?","options":["A router","The sending device only","The payload","A USB port"],"correct":0,"explain":"A router reads the destination address in the packet header and chooses the next pathway."},{"q":"Packets sent using packet switching often arrive out of order. How is the file put back together correctly?","options":["The packet numbers are used to reorder the packets","The packets are sent again in the right order","The router stores all packets until they are in order","The trailer is used to sort them by size"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each packet has a packet number in its header. The receiving device uses these numbers to reorder the packets."}]},{"id":"8.1.4a","code":"8.1.4a","title":"Sequence","video":null,"concept":"<p><b>Sequence</b> means that statements are carried out <b>one after another in the order they are written</b>. The order matters: changing it can change the result.</p><p>A classic example is <b>swapping two values</b>. You need a temporary variable so no value is lost:</p>","example":"<pre class='ln-pre'>A ← 5\nB ← 9\nTemp ← A      // Temp is 5\nA ← B         // A is 9\nB ← Temp      // B is 5</pre><p>Now A = 9 and B = 5. If the order were wrong, e.g. <code>A ← B</code> then <code>B ← A</code>, both would end up as 9 because the original value of A was overwritten before it was saved.</p>","checks":[{"q":"What does sequence mean in programming?","options":["Statements are run one after another in the order written","Statements are repeated until a condition is true","One of several statements is chosen","A block of code is given a name"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sequence is the default flow from the first statement to the last."},{"q":"After <code>A ← 2</code>, <code>B ← 7</code>, <code>A ← B</code>, <code>B ← A</code>, what are the values of A and B?","options":["A is 7 and B is 7","A is 7 and B is 2","A is 2 and B is 7","A is 2 and B is 2"],"correct":0,"explain":"After A ← B, A holds 7. Then B ← A copies 7 again, so both are 7."}]}],"questions":[{"type":"mcq","q":"What is the 8-bit binary number 10101100 in denary?","options":["172","184","108","220"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sum the place values of the 1 bits: 128 + 32 + 8 + 4 = 172.","topic":"Base Conversions"},{"type":"mcq","q":"What is the denary number 189 written as an 8-bit binary integer?","options":["10111101","10111110","11000101","10110101"],"correct":0,"explain":"189 = 128 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 1, so the 1 bits go in those positions: 10111101.","topic":"Base Conversions"},{"type":"mcq","q":"What is the 8-bit binary number 10111110 written in hexadecimal?","options":["BE","BF","AE","CE"],"correct":0,"explain":"Split the byte into two 4-bit nibbles: 1011 = B, 1110 = E, giving BE.","topic":"Base Conversions"},{"type":"mcq","q":"Adding the 8-bit binary integers 11011000 and 01101101 produces a 9-bit result. What is that result?","options":["1 0100 0101","0 0100 0101","1 1011 1101","1 0011 0101"],"correct":0,"explain":"Adding column by column with carries gives 1 0100 0101 (325 in denary).","topic":"Binary Addition & Overflow"},{"type":"mcq","q":"Why does adding 11011000 and 01101101 cause an overflow error if the result must be stored in a single 8-bit register?","options":["The true sum (325) needs 9 bits, but an 8-bit register can only hold values up to 255","Binary addition cannot handle a carry out of the final column","One of the two 8-bit numbers being added is actually a negative two's complement value","8-bit registers cannot store a byte that contains three 1 bits in a row"],"correct":0,"explain":"325 exceeds the maximum value an 8-bit register can represent (255), so the 9th bit is lost — this is overflow.","topic":"Binary Addition & Overflow"}]},"as":{"name":"AS Level Computer Science","code":"9618","lessonTotal":75,"questionTotal":135,"lessons":[{"id":"1.2.4a","code":"1.2.4","title":"Sound: sampling rate, sampling resolution and file size","video":null,"concept":"<p>Sound is an <b>analogue</b> signal: a wave whose value changes continuously. To store it, a computer <b>samples</b> it: the <b>size (amplitude) of the wave is measured at regular intervals</b>, and each measurement is stored as a <b>binary number</b>, in sequence.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Sampling rate</td><td>the number of samples taken <b>each second</b>, in hertz (Hz). 44.1 kHz means 44 100 samples every second</td></tr><tr><td>Sampling resolution</td><td>the number of <b>bits used to store each sample</b> (2 bytes = 16 bits, so 65 536 possible values)</td></tr></table><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>If you &hellip;</th><th>Accuracy of the sound</th><th>File size</th></tr><tr><td>increase the sampling rate</td><td>better: more of the wave is captured</td><td>bigger</td></tr><tr><td>increase the sampling resolution</td><td>better: each value is more exact, smaller quantisation errors</td><td>bigger</td></tr><tr><td>increase the duration</td><td>no change</td><td>bigger</td></tr><tr><td>decrease the sampling rate or resolution</td><td>worse</td><td>smaller</td></tr></table><p><b>File size (bits)</b> = sampling rate &times; sampling resolution &times; time in seconds.</p>","example":"<p><b>10 seconds at 44 100 Hz with 16-bit samples:</b> 44 100 &times; 16 &times; 10 = 7 056 000 bits = 882 000 bytes, about 0.88 MB.</p><p>An 8-bit recording of the same sound would be <b>half</b> the size, but with only 256 possible levels it is less accurate, so the sound is rougher.</p><p><b>'Must be clear which resolution is being described'</b>: when comparing 2 bytes with 8 bits, say which recording each point is about.</p>","checks":[{"q":"What does a sampling rate of 8000 Hz mean?","options":["8000 samples are taken every second","Each sample uses 8000 bits","The sound lasts 8000 seconds","The sound is 8000 times louder"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sampling rate is samples per second."},{"q":"What is sampling resolution?","options":["The number of bits used to store each sample","The number of samples per second","The length of the recording","The number of speakers"],"correct":0,"explain":"The more bits, the more exact each measurement."},{"q":"Which change makes a sound recording smaller in file size?","options":["Decrease the sampling resolution","Increase the sampling rate","Increase the recording time","Increase the number of bits per sample"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fewer bits per sample means less data."},{"q":"A sound is recorded with 16-bit samples. How many different amplitude values can each sample have?","options":["65 536","16","256","1024"],"correct":0,"explain":"2^16 = 65 536."},{"type":"text","q":"Describe what is meant by a sampling rate of 44.1 kHz. [1]","groups":[["44100","44 100","44.1 thousand","44,100"],["times each second","per second","every second","a second"]],"need":2,"model":"The sound wave is measured 44 100 times each second (44 100 samples are taken every second).","explain":"The sound wave is measured 44 100 times each second (44 100 samples are taken every second)."},{"type":"pp","src":"9618/12 May/June 2026 · Question 4","explain":"Sampling rate = samples per second. Sampling resolution = bits per sample: more bits means a bigger file and a more accurate sound. When you compare two recordings, say which one each statement is about.","parts":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"kind":"text","lines":3,"code":false,"q":"<p>A sound file has a sampling rate of 44.1 kHz. Describe what is meant by a sampling rate of 44.1 kHz.</p>","points":[{"t":"The sound wave is measured 44 100 times each second // 44 100 samples are taken every second","k":[["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","each second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","per second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","every second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","a second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","in one second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","one second"],["re:44\\.?\\s?1\\s?(k|thousand)|44\\s?100|44,100","hz"]]}],"max":1,"scheme":"<p>1 mark for:</p><ul><li>The sound wave is measured 44 100 times each second // 44 100 samples are taken every second</li></ul><p><i>Accept appropriate alternative words for measured, including 'sampled'. Accept 44.1 thousand instead of 44100.</i></p>"},{"label":"(b)","marks":3,"kind":"text","lines":6,"code":false,"q":"<p>A student creates a recording of a sound with a sampling resolution of 2 bytes. The student creates a second recording of the same sound but with a sampling resolution of 8 bits. Explain how the two sound recordings and the two sound files will be affected by the different sampling resolutions.</p>","points":[{"t":"The resolution of 2 bytes (16 bits) will have more bits per sample","k":["more bits","16 bits","greater number of bits","higher number of bits","2 bytes"]},{"t":"... which will result in a larger file size","k":["larger","bigger","more storage","greater file size","bigger file","more data","increase"]},{"t":"... but the digital sound will be more accurate to the original","k":["more accurate","better quality","more exact","closer to the original","higher quality","more detail","clearer"]},{"t":"... because a greater range / more exact amplitudes can be recorded per sample, with smaller quantisation errors","k":["range","quantisation","quantization","more exact","more values","more levels","more possible","amplitude"]}],"max":3,"scheme":"<p>1 mark each to a maximum of 3:</p><ul><li>The resolution of 2 bytes (16 bits) will have more bits per sample</li><li>... which will result in a larger file size</li><li>... but the digital sound will be more accurate to the original</li><li>... because a greater range / more exact amplitudes can be recorded per sample, with smaller quantisation errors</li></ul><p><i>Must be clear which resolution is being described. Allow reverse arguments: the resolution of 8 bits will have fewer bits per sample, which results in a smaller file size.</i></p>"}]}]}],"questions":[{"type":"paper","topic":"Sound: sampling rate","marks":3,"prompt":"A sound is recorded digitally.\n(a) State what is meant by the sampling rate. [1]\n(b) State one effect of increasing the sampling rate on the quality of the recording and one effect on the file size. [2]","markPoints":[{"text":"(a) The number of samples (measurements of the sound wave) taken per second","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["sample","measurement"],["per second","each second","every second","a second","hertz","hz"]],"needCount":2}},{"text":"(b) Higher quality: the recording is closer to / a more accurate copy of the original sound","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["quality","accurate","accuracy","closer","faithful","better"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"(b) The file size increases (more data is stored)","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["larger","bigger","more storage","more memory","more data","file size increases","increases the file","increase in file size"]],"needCount":1}}],"explanation":"A sample is one measurement of the sound wave. Taking more samples each second records the wave more accurately, but there is more data to store, so the file is larger."}]},"a2":{"name":"A Level (A2) Computer Science","code":"9618","lessonTotal":129,"questionTotal":85,"lessons":[{"id":"17.1a","code":"17.1.1","title":"Why encrypt? Plaintext, ciphertext and keys","video":null,"concept":"<p>When data travels over a public network it can be intercepted by an <b>eavesdropper</b> (a hacker). <b>Encryption</b> cannot stop the data being intercepted, but it makes it <b>unreadable</b> to anyone it is not meant for. Four security aims are involved:</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Aim</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><b>Confidentiality</b></td><td>Only the intended recipient can read the data</td></tr><tr><td><b>Authenticity</b></td><td>The sender is who they claim to be</td></tr><tr><td><b>Integrity</b></td><td>The data arrives unchanged</td></tr><tr><td><b>Non-repudiation</b></td><td>Neither sender nor receiver can deny taking part</td></tr></table><ul><li><b>Plaintext</b>: the original data before encryption.</li><li><b>Ciphertext</b>: the result of putting plaintext through an encryption algorithm with a key.</li><li>An <b>encryption key</b> is a value used by the algorithm; decryption turns ciphertext back into plaintext with the matching <b>decryption key</b>.</li><li>A <b>block cipher</b> encrypts a group of bits (e.g. 128 bits) at a time; a <b>stream cipher</b> encrypts bit by bit as they arrive. In <b>block chaining</b> each plaintext block is XORed with the previous ciphertext block before encryption, so identical plaintext blocks do not give identical ciphertext.</li></ul>","example":"<p>plaintext <code>HELLO</code> → encryption algorithm + key → ciphertext (looks like random characters) → sent over the internet → decryption algorithm + key → plaintext <code>HELLO</code>.</p><p>A longer key means more possible keys and so a message that takes longer to crack: a 10-digit key has about 10 billion possibilities (cracked in seconds by a modern computer) but a 256-bit key has 2<sup>256</sup>.</p>","checks":[{"q":"What is ciphertext?","options":["Data that has been through an encryption algorithm","The original message before encryption","The key used to encrypt","A digital signature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Plaintext goes in, ciphertext comes out."},{"q":"Does encryption stop data being intercepted?","options":["No, it makes intercepted data meaningless to the eavesdropper","Yes, it blocks all interception","Yes, it makes the cable private","No, and it also makes the data easier to read"],"correct":0,"explain":"Encryption protects confidentiality, not the transmission itself."},{"q":"Which security aim means that data reaches its destination without being changed?","options":["Integrity","Confidentiality","Authenticity","Non-repudiation"],"correct":0,"explain":"Integrity = unchanged data."},{"q":"What does a block cipher do?","options":["Encrypts a group of bits at the same time","Encrypts one bit at a time","Encrypts only the key","Compresses the data"],"correct":0,"explain":"A stream cipher is the bit-by-bit method."},{"type":"text","q":"Explain why data is encrypted before being sent over the internet. [2]","groups":[["intercept","eavesdrop","hacker","read","steal"],["unreadable","meaningless","cannot understand","not understand","make no sense","secure","confidential","private"]],"need":2,"model":"Data sent over a public network can be intercepted by an eavesdropper. Encryption turns it into ciphertext that is meaningless without the key, so it stays confidential.","explain":"Data sent over a public network can be intercepted by an eavesdropper. Encryption turns it into ciphertext that is meaningless without the key, so it stays confidential."}]},{"id":"19.1g","code":"19.1.3","title":"Linked lists","video":null,"concept":"<p>A <b>linked list</b> is a list where each item holds a <b>pointer</b> to the next item, so the items can be stored anywhere and a new one can be added without shifting the others. When it is implemented with two arrays:</p><ul><li><code>MyLinkedList[i]</code> holds the data and <code>MyLinkedListPointers[i]</code> holds the index of the next item (-1 = null pointer, end of list).</li><li><code>StartPointer</code> gives the first item (-1 when the list is empty).</li><li>The unused locations form a second linked list, the <b>heap</b>, starting at <code>HeapStartPointer</code>.</li></ul><pre class='ln-pre'>// Add an item at the start of the list\nPROCEDURE LinkedListAdd(ItemAdd : INTEGER)\n    IF HeapStartPointer = NullPointer THEN\n        OUTPUT \"Linked list full\"\n    ELSE\n        TempPointer ← StartPointer\n        StartPointer ← HeapStartPointer\n        HeapStartPointer ← MyLinkedListPointers[HeapStartPointer]\n        MyLinkedList[StartPointer] ← ItemAdd\n        MyLinkedListPointers[StartPointer] ← TempPointer\n    ENDIF\nENDPROCEDURE</pre><pre class='ln-pre'>FUNCTION Find(ItemSearch : INTEGER) RETURNS INTEGER\n    ItemPointer ← StartPointer\n    WHILE ItemPointer &lt;&gt; NullPointer AND MyLinkedList[ItemPointer] &lt;&gt; ItemSearch DO\n        ItemPointer ← MyLinkedListPointers[ItemPointer]\n    ENDWHILE\n    RETURN ItemPointer    // -1 if not found\nENDFUNCTION</pre><pre class='ln-pre'>PROCEDURE LinkedListDelete(ItemDelete : INTEGER)\n    IF StartPointer = NullPointer THEN\n        OUTPUT \"Linked list empty\"\n    ELSE\n        Index ← StartPointer\n        OldIndex ← NullPointer\n        WHILE Index &lt;&gt; NullPointer AND MyLinkedList[Index] &lt;&gt; ItemDelete DO\n            OldIndex ← Index\n            Index ← MyLinkedListPointers[Index]\n        ENDWHILE\n        IF Index = NullPointer THEN\n            OUTPUT \"Item not found\"\n        ELSE\n            TempPointer ← MyLinkedListPointers[Index]\n            MyLinkedListPointers[Index] ← HeapStartPointer\n            HeapStartPointer ← Index\n            IF OldIndex = NullPointer THEN\n                StartPointer ← TempPointer\n            ELSE\n                MyLinkedListPointers[OldIndex] ← TempPointer\n            ENDIF\n        ENDIF\n    ENDIF\nENDPROCEDURE</pre><p><b>Note:</b> the delete procedure handles the case where the item to delete is the <b>first</b> item (OldIndex is still the null pointer), by moving StartPointer. The textbook version does not handle this case.</p>","example":"<p>A list with 8 locations after adding 27, 19, 36, 42 and 16 in that order (each new item goes to the front):</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Index</th><th>Data</th><th>Pointer</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>27</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>19</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>36</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>42</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>-1</td></tr></table><p>startPointer = <b>4</b>, heapStartPointer = <b>5</b></p><p>Reading from the start pointer: <b>16, 42, 36, 19, 27</b>.</p><p>After adding <b>18</b>:</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Index</th><th>Data</th><th>Pointer</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>27</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>19</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>36</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>42</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>18</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>-1</td></tr></table><p>startPointer = <b>5</b>, heapStartPointer = <b>6</b></p><p>List: <b>18, 16, 42, 36, 19, 27</b>.</p><p>After deleting <b>36</b>: its location is returned to the heap and the pointer of the item before it in the chain (42) is changed to skip it:</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Index</th><th>Data</th><th>Pointer</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>27</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>19</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>42</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>18</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>-1</td></tr></table><p>startPointer = <b>5</b>, heapStartPointer = <b>2</b></p><p>List: <b>18, 16, 42, 19, 27</b>.</p>","checks":[{"q":"In a linked list held in two arrays, what does a pointer value of -1 mean?","options":["There is no next item (end of the list)","The item is deleted","The list is full","The item is the largest"],"correct":0,"explain":"-1 is used as the null pointer."},{"q":"What is the order of the list after adding 27, 19, 36, 42, 16 and then 18 (each added at the front)?","options":["18, 16, 42, 36, 19, 27","16, 42, 36, 19, 27","18, 27, 19, 36, 42, 16","16, 18, 42, 36, 19, 27"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each new item becomes the start of the list."},{"q":"After adding the six items above, where is 18 stored (its index)?","options":["5","4","6","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Locations are taken from the heap in order 0, 1, 2, ...; 18 is the sixth item added, so it is at index 5."},{"q":"What does the heap in a linked list hold?","options":["The unused locations, linked together","The deleted program files","The sorted data","The first item of the list"],"correct":0,"explain":"The free list starts at HeapStartPointer."},{"q":"When an item is deleted from a linked list, what happens to its location?","options":["It is added to the heap of free locations","It is filled with the next item","It is moved to the end of the array","It is never reused"],"correct":0,"explain":"Its pointer is set to the old heap start and HeapStartPointer is set to its index."},{"type":"text","q":"Explain why a linked list can be easier to insert items into than an array. [2]","groups":[["no need to shift","not shift","no shifting","don't need to move","not move","without moving","does not need to move","not have to move","shifted","shift"],["pointer","pointers","change","update","adjust","next"]],"need":2,"model":"Items do not need to be shifted along to make room; only the pointers are changed to include the new item.","explain":"Items do not need to be shifted along to make room; only the pointers are changed to include the new item."}]}],"questions":[{"type":"paper","topic":"Symmetric and asymmetric encryption","marks":3,"prompt":"Describe how symmetric encryption differs from asymmetric encryption. [3]","markPoints":[{"text":"Symmetric encryption uses the same key to encrypt and to decrypt","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["same key","single key","one key","identical key","shared key","shared secret"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"Asymmetric encryption uses a pair of different keys: a public key and a private key","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["public"],["private"]],"needCount":2}},{"text":"The public key encrypts and only the matching private key can decrypt (so the decryption key is never shared)","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["public key"],["decrypt"],["private key"]],"needCount":3}}],"explanation":"Symmetric: one shared secret key does both jobs, so the key must be exchanged safely. Asymmetric: the public key can be given to anyone and used to encrypt; only the owner's private key can decrypt."}]}};
